Revision history for Selecto

Unreleased
    - Date shortcuts are measured from the database's current date: without
      an explicit today, Selecto::DateShortcut->expression returns the new
      date_shortcut expression, which PostgreSQL and DuckDB compile against
      CURRENT_DATE (or today in the query's use_timezone), so the session
      time zone decides when a day begins. Other adapters still bind the
      server's date. An epoch column now compares as an instant. Shortcut
      bounds are defined once, as terms(), for Perl and SQL alike.
    - Bounded child collections accept a formatted child date
      ({key, expression => datetime_format(...)}); other child expressions
      are still refused.
    - A canned text control with multiple => 1 takes up to 100 values
      separated by whitespace, commas or semicolons (such as pasted VINs)
      and matches a row that starts with any of them.
    - datetime_format takes a timezone option, which shows that one value in
      an IANA zone without localizing the query's filters, and two display
      formats: us_date (10/08/2026) and us_datetime (10/08/2026 2:30 PM),
      on PostgreSQL and DuckDB. A day_minute format (2026-10-08 14:30)
      sorts in date order, so it is also offered for grouping.
    - Canned detail views may select an aliased datetime_format of an
      entity-grain field, and related collections may format a child date
      with {key, expression}. The formatted field joins the detail GROUP BY.
    - Faster API query responses, byte for byte the same. EngineHandler->query
      checks the adapter result (its traversal limits and every cell's
      bytes) and the response's exact JSON bytes
      (Selecto::API::ResponsePolicy->check_json) in one pass each, without a
      method call per cell, and Selecto::API->request skips its second walk
      of the result (public_data's traversal check) and canonical_json's
      per-value validation when the value cannot fail them. Each limit
      compares a running total that only grows, so the single pass decides
      exactly what the previous checks decided; a result or response that
      reaches a limit, or holds anything the single pass does not take
      (blessed or unsupported references, cycles, and in check_json numbers
      that are not integers), goes through the previous checks, which raise
      the same error. Limits, defaults, statuses and response bytes are
      unchanged. 100,000 rows of 10 columns (median of 5 interleaved runs,
      RAM PostgreSQL): EngineHandler->query with a Mojo::JSON encode 4.81 s
      to 1.44 s (arrays) and 6.87 s to 2.09 s (objects); Selecto::API->request
      11.1 s to 2.9 s; peak RSS unchanged. A 50-row objects page: 4.5 to 1.9 ms
      (handler), 9.0 to 3.1 ms (Selecto::API).
    - Breaking: Selecto::PostgreSQL returns the values DBD::Pg fetched,
      untouched, unless canonical values are asked for. Engine::all,
      Engine::stream (bounded too) and Engine::projection_sum take
      canonical_values => 1 per call, and the adapter takes
      canonical_values => 1 (default 0) to make them the default again
      (a call's canonical_values => 0 then asks for driver values). What a
      host that does not ask sees, per PostgreSQL type (DBD::Pg 3): numeric
      keeps its scale ("10.500", not "10.5"); real and
      double precision are Perl numbers, so JSON numbers (1.5, 1e+20),
      instead of their Perl strings ("1.5", "1e+20", "10000000000",
      "Inf"); timestamp and timestamptz keep the server text
      ("2024-01-01 10:00:00+00" or "2024-01-01 04:00:00-06" under the
      session TimeZone, not "2024-01-01T10:00:00"); integers and booleans
      (already numbers and 1/0), text, date, json text and the rest are
      unchanged. To keep the old behaviour:
          Selecto->adapter(postgresql => (dbh => $dbh, canonical_values => 1));
      or $engine->all($query, canonical_values => 1). The API query and
      resource handlers, canned pages, co-domain lookups and export_scalars
      ask for canonical values, so their responses are unchanged. Other
      adapters always return canonical values. A Selecto::PostgreSQL
      subclass that overrides execute_query is not sent the option unless
      its supports('canonical_values') is true.
    - Canonical values are decoded in Perl by default, as before: a host
      that asks for them gets exactly the values 0.2.2 returned, and dates
      and timestamps follow the session DateStyle (a non-ISO DateStyle such
      as SQL, Postgres or German shows through, as it always has). Integer
      columns that DBD::Pg fetched as Perl numbers skip int() on Perl 5.36
      and newer.
    - Opt-in canonical_sql => 1 (default 0) has PostgreSQL format the
      canonical values of fields declared decimal (PostgreSQL 13+), date,
      naive_datetime and utc_datetime in the outermost select list: trim_scale
      for numeric, and TO_CHAR for dates and timestamps, in the session
      TimeZone, with the offset from TO_CHAR's OF unless it is +00. Its
      dates and timestamps are always the ISO form in the session time zone,
      whatever the DateStyle; under ISO they equal the Perl decode (BC,
      infinities, fractions and historic offsets with seconds included). The
      declared type chooses the columns and the column's PostgreSQL type
      (CASE on pg_typeof) the formatter, so a mis-declared temporal kind or a
      date kept as text is unchanged. Columns PostgreSQL does not format
      (integers, floats, booleans, aggregates and other expressions,
      computed fields, ordered rollups, set operations) are decoded in Perl.
      A float or integer column declared decimal comes out as its
      PostgreSQL text ("1e+20", "Infinity"; "7" as a string). ORDER BY and
      GROUP BY keep the underlying expressions. Streams, bounded streams and
      BoundedQuery format the same columns (so a bounded result's cell-size
      guard measures the canonical text). It is off by default because the
      Perl decode measured faster at every result size (100,000 rows: 154 ms
      in Perl, 213 ms in SQL), and PostgreSQL parses and plans the longer
      statement on every call without the statement cache (roughly 30-50
      microseconds per formatted datetime column; point reads were 100-150
      microseconds slower). With canonical_sql off, compile records nothing
      for it.
    - getResource takes an optional date_format query parameter (iso8601,
      the default and the previous output; rfc3339_millis; epoch_seconds;
      epoch_milliseconds) that formats every temporal field in UTC,
      compiled into the SQL through datetime_format. Under the non-default
      formats a date is midnight UTC and a naive datetime is UTC wall time;
      epochs round down; null stays null. Any other value is
      invalid_api_query with details {parameter => 'date_format'} (public
      error details may now carry parameter). Documented in the OpenAPI
      document (an enum), Selecto::API and Selecto::API::EngineHandler;
      EngineHandler->resource_date_formats lists the values.
    - Canonical direct associations infer tenant equality from both declared
      relation tenant fields, including nested edges, correlated collections,
      computed EXISTS and PostgreSQL lateral lookups. Explicit complete pairs
      retain precedence; either partial pair is invalid_domain. Tenantless
      targets, legacy explicit scope and through contracts retain their policy.
    - Explicit hidden column metadata now withholds the field from every
      public surface using Domain::field_is_public, matching internal and
      redacted fields. API query roles and named routes refuse hidden_field
      (403), while trusted Engine reads remain available.
    - Public API queries enforce explicit filterable/sortable field roles on
      direct intent and named query-library routes, with invalid_field (422).
      Optional bounded group_by enforces public/groupable fields and refuses
      nongrouped selections or orderings before database execution.
    - Public API query pages publish has_more from one extra fetched row and
      return at most the requested limit. Zero-sized pages fetch no rows.
    - Opt-in PostgreSQL statement cache. Selecto::PostgreSQL takes
      statement_cache (default 0) and statement_cache_size (default 256).
      When on, execute_query and executed writes reuse one statement handle
      per SQL text and connection (kept with prepare_cached under a private
      attribute, so it is freed with the handle), and DBD::Pg runs them as
      named server-side statements. Least recently used handles beyond the
      bound are deallocated, only right after a statement succeeded. A lost
      statement (26000) or changed result type (0A000) is prepared again
      once outside a transaction; inside one the original error and
      SQLSTATE stand. New Selecto::PostgreSQL::StatementCache (forget,
      count). Unchanged with it off (the default): every call prepares a new
      handle as before. Streams, insert admission and bounded-write probes
      always prepare afresh. Not for transaction-mode poolers before
      PgBouncer 1.21.
    - Each domain action with preconditions or a transition gets a boolean
      can_<action> column, true when the row meets them, with Yes/No filter
      choices, so the API and Explorer can show and filter the rows an
      action could run on. Actions opt out with prerequisite_column false.
      Domain::without_action_prerequisites removes the columns of actions a
      caller cannot see, and Selecto::Action->prerequisites returns the
      guards without planning a target.
    - Selecto::API serves GET .../resources/{id} (getResource) when constructed
      with resources => 1; a resource's aggregate_version is its ETag, and
      resource_not_found maps to 404.
    - Selecto::API::EngineHandler reads one resource by primary key with
      resource(), and publishes a virtual, read-only aggregate_version field
      through an optional host versioner. Resources and queries return it only
      when it is requested.
    - API subtables may be ordered by an internal child primary key. The key
      only orders the bounded collection and is never returned.
    - Trusted adapters may admit native flat array parameters under finite
      bind-slot, element and conservative wire-byte budgets. Other adapters
      retain scalar-only parameter admission.
    - Query compilation admits Selecto::Query subclasses used by typed SQL
      pattern builders while retaining depth, size, cycle and reference checks.
    - Native adapters without a DBI handle keep normalized query_error
      responses when their state is locked. SQLSTATE inspection now checks
      whether a handle exists before reading it.
    - Database failures keep the driver's SQLSTATE. A query_error from
      normalize_error now carries details.sqlstate and details.category
      (unique_violation, foreign_key_violation, not_null_violation,
      check_violation, query_canceled, serialization_failure,
      deadlock_detected, or database_error), as in the Elixir library, so a
      host can tell a lock timeout (55P03), deadlock (40P01) or
      serialization failure (40001) apart and retry. Before, the code was
      dropped, and the write's rollback cleared it from the handle. Insert
      admission and Selecto::Stream now normalize before finishing or
      closing the statement, which also cleared it. New
      Selecto::Adapter->driver_sqlstate. Unchanged: the code (query_error),
      the message, details.cause, the specific constraint errors, and API
      responses, whose details allow-list leaves sqlstate out outside debug.
    - A missing tenant scope is refused with 403 through Selecto::API, as
      in the other runtimes. Selecto::API::error_status now returns 403 for
      missing_tenant_scope (was 422), so Selecto::API->request answers a
      handler error with that code and no status with 403, as does the
      documented host guard that passes status => error_status($code).
      Changed responses: EngineHandler->query or ->write on a tenant_field
      domain whose engine has no trusted tenant boundary (and any other
      missing_tenant_scope a handler returns without a status) now answer
      403 instead of 422. Unchanged: the code, the body (details {} and the
      redacted message outside debug_sql), a status the handler sets, the
      Selecto::Error thrown by the engine and handler, every other code.
    - Queries that name a hidden field are refused as hidden_field (403
      through Selecto::API), as in the other runtimes. Changed responses,
      all from EngineHandler->query: a select entry (plain, object or
      subtable), projection, view, segment, view segment, named ordering,
      view ordering, order_by or filter that reaches an internal or
      redact_fields field used to fail with field_not_public and now fails
      with hidden_field (details {field}; message "Field is not exposed.");
      through Selecto::API a handler error without a status now answers
      hidden_field with 403 instead of 422 (new Selecto::API::error_status).
      Within select, an unknown field or another invalid entry is now
      reported before a hidden one, and no type or association check runs
      on a hidden field (a date format on a hidden text field is now
      hidden_field, not invalid_api_query). Unchanged: unknown fields
      (unknown_field), write assignments, write filters and returning
      fields (field_not_public), conditional choice filters reading
      internal fields (field_not_public), every other code and status.
    - CSV and TSV exports follow the certified cross-runtime cell rules
      (api_export_rules) instead of Text::CSV's defaults. Changed export
      bytes: a cell is no longer quoted for a space alone ("two words",
      " ", "trail "); a cell with any non-ASCII character is now always
      quoted (before, only when its UTF-8 bytes included 0x7F-0xA0, so
      "café"/"Renée" were bare while "東京" was quoted); control characters
      (0x01-0x08, 0x0B, 0x0C, 0x0E-0x1F, 0x7F) no longer force quotes, and a
      NUL is written as is instead of the escaped "0; a formula lead after
      leading Unicode White_Space ("  =1+1", "\x{A0}=x", VT/FF then "=")
      is now neutralized with a leading "'". Headers follow the same rules.
      Unchanged: separators, quotes, tab/CR/LF, leading =,+,-,@ and
      tab/CR/LF, nulls, booleans, numbers, nested JSON, CRLF row endings.
    - Opt-in export scalars: Engine->all($query, export_scalars => 1) and
      EngineHandler->query($engine, $body, export_scalars => 1) return
      NUMERIC as PostgreSQL's text at the column scale ("533.10",
      "7152.00", "0.0000"), booleans as JSON booleans and json/jsonb as
      decoded values, for exports. Only the PostgreSQL adapter supports it;
      other adapters refuse it (unsupported_feature). Without the option,
      results are unchanged.
    - Faster large IN lists with the same SQL, parameters, limits and
      refusals: operation budgets admit a list of values in one pass (each
      value still checked in order, each maximum looked up once), recognise
      core booleans without a JSON::PP::is_bool call per scalar, and count
      bytes without a bytes::length call; association discovery no longer
      walks plain IN members. A 500-value IN filter now composes and
      compiles in about 1.7 ms instead of 3.4 ms locally.
    - Faster query composition, compilation and write governance with the
      same resource limits, thresholds and error codes: inlined operation
      budget comparisons (the breach path is unchanged), one admission per
      query under Engine->compile instead of two, a single deep copy in write
      command derivation, no whole-contract copy to read one section, no
      copies of field metadata or the applied query library for internal
      reads, and a shared default limits policy.
    - Faster bounded results: Selecto::BoundedQuery (canned pages, component
      result pages) fetches min(max_rows + 1, 100) guarded rows per round
      trip instead of one, and QueryBudget->check(defer_rearm => 1) lets a
      bounded stream refresh the server statement timeout right before each
      FETCH instead of once per row. The wall deadline is still checked on
      every row and the server deadline before every fetch. 500 guarded rows
      locally: 64 ms -> 25 ms; a 50-row page 8.1 ms -> 4.4 ms.
    - Bounded PostgreSQL streams fetch fetch_size rows per round trip
      (default 1, as before) instead of always one, read column types once
      and decode each batch column by column. Every existing bounded caller
      passes fetch_size => 1, so nothing changes until a caller asks for more;
      docs/security-boundaries.md describes the new bound.
    - Faster query results: execute_query fetches with fetchall_arrayref and
      PostgreSQL decodes column by column, choosing each column's type branch
      once instead of per cell. Results are identical (t/decode_rows.t checks
      every cell against _decode). On the performance lane's 5,000 x 20
      result this halves query time (70 ms to 34 ms). Adapters with their own
      _decode keep the per-cell path; one whose _decode is the identity skips
      decoding.
    - Expression->from_filter_ast reads a decoded JSON boolean comparison value
      or in member as the literal 1 or 0 instead of refusing it as a reference.
    - 0.2.2 security remediation: bound complete filter/value-expression trees,
      scalar and whole-operation parameter bytes, exact rule numeric work,
      non-backtracking ASCII patterns, and importer preview amplification.
    - Refuse unverifiable typed governed inserts before DML; use verified
      SQLite/PostgreSQL storage types, rounding and collations. Fix computed
      IN operand binding order. See docs/typed-insert-admission.md.
    - Bound ordinary UI/canned result consumption and final API JSON/CSV/TSV/
      XLSX bytes. SQL diagnostics and full-domain publication now default off;
      trusted debug/publication configuration is required to enable them.
    - Security: apply trusted finite budgets to query-library expansion, CSV
      inspection, action targets, and nested public API results. Oversized
      nested collections are refused before returning partial data.
    - Security: remove hidden field values and matched-row error counts;
      enforce public co-domain lookup fields and authority-specific file hold
      release with audit-before-mutation behavior.
    - Security: preserve selected action upsert identity, require declared
      conflict targets on all governed paths, and refuse MySQL/MariaDB upserts
      whose requested identity cannot be guaranteed. Separate authored
      system-now instructions from caller literals.
    - Security: preserve SELECT/JOIN positional parameter order, require
      concrete public non-bulk write targets, and bound broad PostgreSQL/SQLite
      writes before DML. Other adapters refuse unsupported broad writes.
    - Add scoped database execution budgets and verified bounded PostgreSQL
      cursor / SQLite streaming for consumers. See docs/security-boundaries.md
      for migration requirements, defaults and evidence limits.
    - A joins entry that is not an object is refused with invalid_domain
      ("join <name> must be an object") instead of being silently ignored,
      as the Ruby, .NET and Python cores already do.
    - Security: the permissive write policy is removed. Strict is the only
      behaviour: an engine refuses (write_policy_missing) a write to a
      domain without writes.operations, or without writes.fields for
      anything but a delete, and a graph edge without its relationship
      domain. Selecto::Engine->new and from_registry still accept
      write_policy => 'strict', but any other value, including
      'permissive', throws invalid_write_policy. Previously
      write_policy => 'permissive' let a domain without a writes section be
      written. Code that relied on it must declare a writes contract.
    - Selecto::Query->retarget($path, strategy => 'in' | 'exists') moves a
      query's row grain from the domain root to the relation at an
      association path. Earlier filters become the context: the result is
      the distinct target rows the root query's joined read reaches under
      them and the domain's required predicate. Later filters, grouping,
      ordering, and pagination apply to the target. A tenant-scoped target
      carries the root tenant condition or fails closed.
    - The domain retarget section may declare targets (with label and
      default_selected) and a default_target; declared targets form an
      allow-list. Selecto::Retarget exposes the target and relation domains.
    - Security: Selecto::CannedPage->new refuses (invalid_canned_page) a
      definition that names a field the domain withholds from public
      surfaces (redact_fields at any level, or an internal column; see
      Domain::field_is_public) anywhere: the entity key, the dataset
      predicate, view selections (aliases, expressions and collection
      children, filters and orderings), groups and orderings, and control
      fields and label fields. The message names the part and the field
      ("view list references redacted field secret_token") and details
      carry {field, view | control | dataset}. Previously such a page ran
      and showed the field.
    - Security: Selecto::CannedPage->run asserts the tenant boundary for
      every engine, not only a Selecto::Engine. An engine without
      assert_tenant_boundary is refused (missing_tenant_scope) on a
      tenant_field domain; previously a delegating engine skipped the check
      and read every tenant.
    - Security: action grants expire 300 seconds after grant_action unless
      expires_in says otherwise, and expires_in may not exceed 3600
      (invalid_action_grant). Previously a grant without expires_in never
      expired.
    - Security: the API query handler refuses (field_not_public) a
      requested or view segment that reads an internal or redacted field and
      a named ordering that sorts by one. A components.filter_choices
      conditional filter whose fields are not all public accepts only its
      declared choice values and no null test.
    - Security: an update may not set the tenant field to a tenant the
      domain's required tenant predicate excludes, or to a computed value
      (tenant_mismatch); previously it could move a row to another tenant
      when the field was updatable.
    - Security: MySQL and MariaDB refuse tenant-scoped upserts
      (unsupported_scope_predicate): ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE fires on any
      unique key, so a primary-key collision could update another tenant's
      row.
    - SQL Server escapes [ in starts_with, text_contains and ends_with
      patterns, so it no longer opens a character class.
    - Expression->from_filter_ast refuses filters nested more than 64 levels
      deep (invalid_query).
    - Read compiles are linear in the size of an expression: grouped
      expressions are matched through memoized structural digests, and not
      looked up at all when a query has none. A 7,500-deep predicate took
      about 110 seconds to compile and now takes under 0.1.
    - Selecto::Expression->field_references (formerly private to
      CannedPage) lists every field path an expression reads.
    - Security (TW-05): a write with trusted tenants (the engine tenant or
      a required tenant predicate) may only reference a parent row of one of
      those tenants. References are writes.constraints.foreign_keys
      (references.tenant_field names the parent's tenant column, or false for
      a shared relation) and the owner keys of direct to-one associations.
      Each assigned reference becomes an EXISTS guard in the write statement
      (insert and upsert through SELECT ... WHERE, update in its WHERE, both
      MERGE branches on SQL Server); a refused write fails with
      cardinality_mismatch even without expected_count, the same as a
      missing parent. A reference whose tenancy the domain does not record
      fails closed on a tenant-scoped write (invalid_domain,
      foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared). Write::Command carries the
      guards (foreign_key_guards); the engine sets them on every governed
      command.

0.2.0  2026-09-30
    - First CPAN release: governed domains, queries, portable writes,
      governed actions, and database adapters for PostgreSQL, MySQL,
      MariaDB, SQL Server, SQLite, and DuckDB.
    - Licensed under the Artistic License 2.0.
    - utc_datetime columns accept "storage": "naive_utc" for zone-less
      timestamps that hold UTC (Rails datetime columns). On PostgreSQL and
      DuckDB such a column is read as (column AT TIME ZONE 'UTC') wherever
      it is used as an instant: under use_timezone and in the iso8601,
      rfc3339_millis, epoch and timezone_offset formats. Other adapters
      accept the hint and compile as before. Domain::field_storage reports
      it.
    - Managed writes no longer commit or roll back a transaction the host
      opened. When the DBI handle is inside a transaction (AutoCommit off
      after begin_work, an AutoCommit => 0 handle, or a raw BEGIN the
      server reports on PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB or SQL Server) a write,
      batch or graph runs in a savepoint (SAVEPOINT selecto_write_N /
      RELEASE, ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT on failure; SAVE TRANSACTION on SQL
      Server): it commits or rolls back with the host, a failed write
      undoes only itself, and a PostgreSQL transaction stays usable. The
      host now commits an AutoCommit => 0 handle itself. DuckDB has no
      savepoints and refuses such a write with invalid_adapter. A
      transaction that failed to begin is no longer rolled back, begin,
      commit and savepoint failures are Selecto errors, and DuckDB now
      honours transaction_mode => 'external'.
    - SQL adapters accept transaction_handler, a code reference that runs
      each managed write in the host's own transaction API.
    - Every required predicate now guards engine writes, not only reads.
      This deliberately goes beyond the shared protocol's earlier rule
      that required predicates are read scopes (previously only a
      tenant_field domain without writes.scope.tenant applied its required
      predicate to writes). A predicate over root fields is ANDed into
      every update and delete, so they match only rows inside it and
      expected_count counts only those rows; inserted rows must satisfy it
      (query_rule_violation, or query_rule_not_evaluable when a value it
      reads is not assigned); and upsert is refused with
      query_enforcement_unsupported_operation ("upsert is not supported on
      a domain with a required predicate"). A predicate that reads any
      association field refuses every write on the domain with
      query_rule_unsupported_field ("association fields are not portable
      write guards", details relation and the sorted association field
      paths); reads
      are unaffected. writes.scope.tenant still applies and both must
      hold. This covers execute_write, preview_write, write_command,
      execute_batch, the root of execute_graph, preview_action and
      execute_action. The new Engine::required_write_guard exposes the
      check, and Selecto::API::EngineHandler now defers to it: its upsert
      refusal uses the engine message, and write_command no longer puts
      the required predicate on the returned command, so the engine
      applies it exactly once.
